    serviceSlug: 'cloud-devops',
    category: 'Cloud & DevOps',
    date: 'October 10, 2026',
    readTime: '9 min read',
    content: '<h2>The Fear of Friday Deployments</h2><p>In many companies, deploying code is a scary event. It involves SSHing into servers, manually pulling code, and hoping it doesn\'t break. DevOps aims to make deployments boring.</p><h3>Containerization with Docker</h3><p>The first step is packaging your app into a Docker container. This ensures that the environment on your laptop is exactly the same as the production server. No more "It works on my machine!"</p><h3>CI/CD with GitHub Actions</h3><p>We set up a pipeline: Every time you push to main, GitHub Actions automatically runs tests, builds the Docker image, and pushes it to a registry.</p><h3>Zero-Downtime Deployment</h3><p>We use a rolling update strategy. Instead of stopping the old server and starting the new one, we spin up the new server first. Once it is healthy, we route traffic to it and shut down the old one. If the new server fails its health check, the deployment is automatically rolled back.</p>'
